<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sutton Hoo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sutton Hoo is a site near Woodbridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with a cemetery of early 7th-century burial mounds. Its great ship burial, excavated in 1939, held a helmet, a gold buckle, shoulder clasps and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the Byzantine world, and is widely thought to be the grave of Rædwald, king of the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, who died around 624. The finds are the richest evidence for the wealth and contacts of East Anglia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo.jpg" alt="Sutton Hoo" width="320" height="119" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Healing · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutton_Hoo_burial_site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sutton Hoo lies on the bank of the tidal estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-deben">River Deben</a>, opposite the town of Woodbridge, which stands 11 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and below the lowest convenient fording place. The estuary formed a way into East Anglia after the end of Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The name derives from Old English, meaning the southern farmstead on a hill shaped like a heel spur.</p><p>The land was farmed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> roundhouses stood there, and again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and Romano-British period, when it was divided into small enclosures. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> withdrew, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> settled in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and East Anglia is regarded by many scholars as a region where this settlement was early and dense.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> cemeteries of the 6th to 7th centuries stand there. They appear as about twenty earth mounds, the tops of which have been flattened by farming. The earliest burials are probably cremations, such as those under Mounds 3 and 4, which held ashes of a man and a horse and of a man, a woman and a horse, with imported objects from the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p>Mound 2 held a chamber burial covered by a small ship. Other graves are those of people who died violently, some by hanging and decapitation, thought to have been executed from the 8th and 9th centuries onwards near a gallows that may have stood on Mound 5.</p><p>4 metres wide, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> had decayed but left its form in the sand. In the chamber lay a crested and masked helmet comparable to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendel">Vendel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valsgarde">Valsgärde</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, a sword with a gold and garnet pommel, a shield, spears, a gold buckle with a hollow chamber, shoulder-clasps, drinking horns from aurochs, a lyre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the Eastern Roman Empire and textiles.</p><p>Scholars believe that Rædwald, the first king of the East Angles, is the most likely person buried in the ship. The finds have prompted comparison with the world of the poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Edith Pretty, owner of the land, had Basil Brown, a self-taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> excavator, dig from June 1938, and national scholars took over when the importance of Mound 1 became clear. Further excavations were led by Rupert Bruce-Mitford of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> from 1965 to 1971 and by Martin Carver from 1983 to 1992. The principal finds are in the British Museum, which received the treasure as a gift from Pretty. The site is in the care of the National Trust, whose visitor centre holds replicas and a reconstruction of the burial chamber.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sutton Hoo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its royal centre lay at Rendlesham in the Deben valley, close to the burial ground at <b>Sutton Hoo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li></ul></section>
